WEBVTT

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.580
A sealed sparkling drink can look unchanged for

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:05.460
hours.

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:07.780
Does that mean the carbon dioxide has stopped

00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:09.860
moving between the liquid and the gas above

00:00:09.940 --> 00:00:10.420
it?

00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:13.620
The surprising answer is that an unchanged mixture

00:00:13.700 --> 00:00:16.420
can hide an ongoing exchange.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.900
We need to separate what stays steady from

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:20.100
what keeps happening.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:24.500
Think first about a book resting flat on

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:25.140
a table.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.860
The table supports its weight, and the book

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:28.900
stays at rest.

00:00:29.460 --> 00:00:32.900
That is a static mechanical equilibrium: the whole

00:00:32.980 --> 00:00:35.620
object is stationary and its forces balance.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.500
It does not mean every particle inside the

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:39.780
book has stopped moving.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:43.580
Chemical equilibrium has a different kind of balance.

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:47.460
In a reversible reaction, reactants form products and

00:00:47.540 --> 00:00:49.060
products form reactants.

00:00:49.700 --> 00:00:52.980
At dynamic equilibrium, those two reaction rates are

00:00:53.060 --> 00:00:54.580
equal and non-zero.

00:00:55.140 --> 00:00:56.940
Neither direction has stopped.

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.620
Because each direction undoes the other at the

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:02.100
same rate, there is no net change in

00:01:02.140 --> 00:01:02.980
the mixture.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:06.100
The two groups in this schematic represent substances

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.300
in one mixture, not separate containers.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.740
Their balanced exchanges show the average behaviour, not

00:01:12.980 --> 00:01:14.500
exact molecular paths.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:18.420
Here are the words that make that distinction

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.300
precise.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.980
Dynamic means the opposing processes continue.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:26.340
Equilibrium means they balance, so there is no

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.900
net change under the stated conditions.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:32.580
A closed system keeps matter inside, although energy

00:01:32.660 --> 00:01:33.700
can cross the boundary.

00:01:34.180 --> 00:01:36.820
Heat can still pass through a sealed bottle.

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:39.860
An open system allows matter to enter or

00:01:39.940 --> 00:01:40.420
leave.

00:01:40.980 --> 00:01:44.340
So closed does not mean insulated, and open

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:47.380
does not automatically mean every measured quantity must

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.100
change.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.580
For this lesson, our model is a reversible

00:01:52.660 --> 00:01:55.940
system in a closed container, at constant temperature

00:01:56.020 --> 00:01:58.900
and volume, given enough time to settle.

00:01:58.940 --> 00:02:01.460
The lid keeps material available for the reverse

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.740
process, but a lid alone does not establish

00:02:04.820 --> 00:02:06.020
equilibrium.

00:02:06.060 --> 00:02:08.820
In the sparkling drink, carbon dioxide can leave

00:02:08.900 --> 00:02:11.220
the liquid and dissolve back into it.

00:02:11.300 --> 00:02:15.220
Once equilibrium is established, these transfers balance.

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.020
Opening the bottle lets carbon dioxide escape into

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:18.980
the room.

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.020
Under those conditions, the drink loses dissolved gas

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.140
overall.

00:02:23.260 --> 00:02:25.540
Some gas can still dissolve back in.

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.780
The problem is the net loss, not the

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:30.500
complete disappearance of the reverse process.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:35.460
Suppose A can turn into B, and B

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:38.260
can turn into A, one particle for one

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:39.140
particle.

00:02:39.460 --> 00:02:41.860
This is a simplified model in a closed,

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:44.700
fixed-volume container at constant temperature.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:48.100
The supplied measurements show four A particles becoming

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.300
B per second, and four B particles becoming

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.980
A per second, averaged over time.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:57.300
The forward process removes A, but the reverse

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:59.940
process replaces A just as quickly.

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:02.340
The same balance keeps B steady.

00:03:02.740 --> 00:03:05.380
So both concentrations remain constant.

00:03:05.540 --> 00:03:07.540
They do not need to match each other.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:09.700
In this model there is more A than

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:10.180
B.

00:03:10.740 --> 00:03:13.780
Equal rates describe the traffic between the groups,

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:15.860
not the number waiting in each group.

00:03:17.867 --> 00:03:20.106
Now suppose we only know that a sealed

00:03:20.187 --> 00:03:23.026
mixture keeps the same colour for ten minutes.

00:03:23.307 --> 00:03:25.706
Is that enough to establish dynamic equilibrium?

00:03:26.347 --> 00:03:28.826
It is consistent with equilibrium, but it does

00:03:28.907 --> 00:03:31.146
not establish ongoing exchange.

00:03:31.627 --> 00:03:33.786
The reaction might simply be too slow to

00:03:33.987 --> 00:03:35.306
detect over that time.

00:03:35.627 --> 00:03:38.586
Being sealed and containing both substances is not

00:03:38.667 --> 00:03:39.386
enough either.

00:03:39.947 --> 00:03:42.026
In our A and B model, the extra

00:03:42.107 --> 00:03:45.706
evidence was conversion continuing in both directions, at

00:03:45.787 --> 00:03:47.626
matching non-zero rates.

00:03:48.267 --> 00:03:50.266
That is why we could justify the dynamic

00:03:50.347 --> 00:03:51.346
explanation.

00:03:51.627 --> 00:03:54.346
A steady observation tells us what we measured.

00:03:54.747 --> 00:03:57.306
The process evidence tells us why it stayed

00:03:57.507 --> 00:03:57.946
steady.

00:03:59.967 --> 00:04:02.126
The main trap is mixing up a rate

00:04:02.207 --> 00:04:03.326
with a concentration.

00:04:03.967 --> 00:04:06.846
Rate describes how fast conversion happens.

00:04:07.087 --> 00:04:09.886
Concentration describes how much of a substance is

00:04:09.967 --> 00:04:11.406
present per volume.

00:04:11.727 --> 00:04:15.006
Dynamic equilibrium requires the forward and reverse rates

00:04:15.087 --> 00:04:19.166
to match; it does not require equal concentrations.

00:04:19.287 --> 00:04:22.126
Nor does it require unequal concentrations.

00:04:22.287 --> 00:04:24.846
Either is possible in a suitable system.

00:04:24.967 --> 00:04:27.646
And a reaction that has apparently finished is

00:04:27.767 --> 00:04:31.086
not automatically an example of static equilibrium.

00:04:31.167 --> 00:04:32.766
It may have run out of a reactant

00:04:32.927 --> 00:04:35.406
or become too slow to observe.

00:04:35.487 --> 00:04:37.966
A still-looking result does not tell us which

00:04:38.047 --> 00:04:39.326
explanation is right.

00:04:41.334 --> 00:04:42.933
Try this with a new mixture.

00:04:43.574 --> 00:04:46.293
C and D interconvert one for one in

00:04:46.334 --> 00:04:49.493
a closed container at constant temperature and volume.

00:04:49.974 --> 00:04:52.453
Their concentrations are different and steady.

00:04:53.094 --> 00:04:56.373
Measurements show both directions converting three particles per

00:04:56.454 --> 00:04:58.373
second, averaged over time.

00:04:59.014 --> 00:05:00.693
Is this dynamic equilibrium?

00:05:01.334 --> 00:05:04.453
Explain what balances and why the concentrations can

00:05:04.534 --> 00:05:04.933
differ.

00:05:05.574 --> 00:05:07.733
Then say what would be missing if we

00:05:07.814 --> 00:05:10.293
had only the steady concentration readings.

00:05:11.014 --> 00:05:13.093
Take a moment, or pause if you would

00:05:13.174 --> 00:05:13.893
like longer.

00:05:25.900 --> 00:05:30.020
Yes, this supplied evidence supports dynamic equilibrium.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.740
Both conversion rates are non-zero and equal, so

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:37.100
each process replaces what the other removes.

00:05:37.140 --> 00:05:40.300
The concentrations can differ because equal traffic does

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:43.660
not require equal amounts in the two groups.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:45.980
If we only had steady readings, we would

00:05:46.100 --> 00:05:49.100
still need evidence that the opposing conversions continue

00:05:49.180 --> 00:05:50.540
and balance.

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:52.860
A change too slow for our measurements could

00:05:52.940 --> 00:05:53.980
look steady as well.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:05:58.800
An unchanged mixture can still be reacting.

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:02.560
At dynamic equilibrium, the forward and reverse reaction

00:06:02.640 --> 00:06:04.960
rates are equal and non-zero.

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:08.880
That balance keeps composition stable under fixed conditions,

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.760
without requiring equal concentrations.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.400
Our model keeps matter inside the system and

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:16.480
allows enough time to settle.

00:06:16.760 --> 00:06:19.840
Closure helps make equilibrium possible; it does not

00:06:19.920 --> 00:06:21.520
prove it has been reached.

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:24.800
And constant measurements alone do not establish ongoing

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:26.160
exchange.

00:06:26.360 --> 00:06:28.880
Next, we will follow a reversible reaction from

00:06:28.960 --> 00:06:31.280
its starting mixture and see how those two

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:32.560
rates come to match.
